::Q1::At a high level, what is the core task used to generate text in an LLM?
{
~Executing a fixed decision tree for every response
~Looking up every sentence in a database
=Predicting what token is likely to come next
~Selecting only from a predefined list of complete answers
} 


::Q2::Why does training-data scale matter?
{
~It prevents models from using tokens
=Large and varied data can provide more patterns for the model to learn
~It eliminates the need for computation~It guarantees every generated statement is true
} 


::Q3::Which statement best describes how LLMs generate responses?
{
=They generate text from learned patterns rather than possessing human-like understanding~They retrieve every answer from a verified database
~They reason exactly like a human
~They always verify facts before responding
}


::Q4::What happens as an LLM generates a sequence of text? 
{
~It stops after predicting the first token
~It chooses one complete answer before processing the prompt
~It converts every token into an IAM policy=It repeatedly predicts subsequent tokens based on the context available to it
}


::Q5::What is a token limit relevant to?
{
~The number of S3 buckets in an account
~The CPU count of an EC2 instance
~The number of AWS users allowed=How much tokenized context and output a model can handle within its limit
}
